          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Anxiety sits in the mental and behavioural chapter. Where would a physical symptom of it, like palpitations, be classified?",
          "answer": "The retrieved sources do not directly say where palpitations caused by anxiety are classified, so I can't give a firm answer from them.\n\nWhat the sources do say:\n\n- **Anxiety disorders:** The ICD-10-CM Chapter 5 summary (doc: coding-icd10-chapter-05-mental) places anxiety, phobias, panic and generalized anxiety disorder in codes F40 to F48. It also says the somatic symptom categories sit in this same range.\n- **Palpitations:** The ICD-10-CM Chapter 9 summary (doc: coding-icd10-chapter-09-circulatory) says palpitations, when no diagnosis has been recorded, stay with the symptoms and abnormal findings in Chapter 18.\n\nTaken together, these suggest that palpitations are first classed as a symptom in Chapter 18, and that the mental health chapter becomes relevant only if a mental disorder is recorded. Which code applies depends on how a clinician documents the case. The sources don't explain that decision for anxiety specifically, so please check the official ICD-10-CM coding guidelines or ask a clinician or medical coder.\n\nIf you're asking because of your own symptoms, a clinician is the right person to discuss them. If palpitations come with chest pain, fainting, or trouble breathing, seek urgent medical care straight away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
